    "narrative": "The Austin Waves lineup is anchored today by the dynamic duo of **Gunnar Henderson** (81.88) and **Kyle Tucker** (67.28). **Gunnar Henderson** is our premier play, benefiting from a favorable matchup against Matthews and an ideal leadoff spot; his projection is further boosted by the pitcher's slow delivery, which creates an prime opportunity for him to exploit the stolen base scarcity in our 5x5 format. Meanwhile, **Kyle Tucker** remains a must-start despite the slightly tougher task against Daniel Lynch IV; his score is buoyed significantly by favorable wind conditions blowing out, which elevates his power potential despite the neutral platoon dynamic. **Drake Baldwin** (67.38) and **Sam Antonacci** (67.67) also land in the \"Strong\" territory, with **Sam Antonacci** capitalizing on an excellent SP skill matchup against Lowder, while **Drake Baldwin** utilizes an advantageous spot in the #2 hole to maximize his overall utility.\n\nOur most difficult decision involved **Nasim Nunez** (52.28) and his battle for the MI slot. While **Nasim Nunez** carries immense value as a stolen base threat, he was nearly pushed to the bench due to an elite hold rate from Peterson and a sub-optimal #9 lineup position that lowers his run-scoring ceiling. Ultimately, his speed profile keeps him active over bench alternatives like **Jacob Wilson** or **Miguel Vargas**, who are currently not slated for starting roles. We are also monitoring the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Wade Meckler** and **Andy Pages**; while both are currently projected for the starting nine, their scores could fluctuate once confirmed batting orders are locked. **Luis Robert Jr.** (52.09) faces a riskier outing today due to a negative platoon split against Mahle, making him a marginal play compared to the efficiency we expect from **Jackson Merrill** (61.47) and **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (63.03) in their respective favorable situational windows. We remain cautious with **Heliot Ramos** (45.65), as his score reflects a difficult matchup against B. Miller, keeping him near our floor for necessary roster coverage.",
